# Postgres connection string. Locally, run `npx prisma dev` and copy the printed
# TCP url here. In production, use your Neon (or other Postgres) connection string.
DATABASE_URL=""

# Auth.js — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=""

# Google Gemini (free tier) — get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=""
GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-flash-latest"

# Max Gemini evaluations one user may run per UTC day. Set to "0" to switch
# evaluations off entirely. Defaults to 5 if unset or not a whole number.
DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT="5"

# Max evaluations across ALL users per UTC day. Commented out = no ceiling.
#
# This is the one that actually protects a shared free-tier key: the per-user limit above
# only governs how fast one account burns through, and accounts are cheap to make. Set it
# before deploying publicly.
#
# Keep it at or under HALF the free-tier requests-per-day published for GEMINI_MODEL:
# this app counts UTC days and Google's quota resets on its own schedule, so the two
# windows overlap and a ceiling of N can allow close to 2N inside one of Google's days.
#
# Left commented rather than empty so that copying this file cannot be mistaken for
# configuring a ceiling of zero, which would refuse every submission.
# GLOBAL_DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT="100"

# Max Gemini calls per minute across all users. Commented out = no rate gate.
#
# The daily ceiling bounds the total; this bounds the burst. Free-tier quotas are
# per-minute as well as per-day, and simultaneous submissions become simultaneous API
# calls. A request refused here costs the user nothing — no daily slot is charged.
# GEMINI_RPM_LIMIT="5"

# Evaluations per UTC day shared by ALL accounts younger than 24 hours. Commented out =
# new accounts are not restricted.
#
# The per-user limit assumes accounts cost something to make. With open registration they
# do not, so an abuser does not spend their own five — they mint accounts and spend
# everyone's. This bounds the payoff rather than the price: a farmed account is always a
# new account, so however many are created they share this one allowance and the rest of
# the ceiling stays for people who were here yesterday.
#
# Enforced only once the day's total passes half the ceiling above. Below that line
# nothing is being contended, so refusing a newcomer would waste quota and lose a real
# person for no gain; above it, this is what keeps a flood of fresh accounts from
# crowding out everyone who was already here.
#
# It does not stop patience. Accounts registered today are established tomorrow, which is
# inherent to any age-based rule — the aim is that farming stops paying off inside a
# session. "0" stops new accounts evaluating at all until they age in.
# NEW_ACCOUNT_DAILY_LIMIT="20"

# What ONE account may evaluate on its first day, in place of DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT above.
# Commented out = new accounts get the ordinary quota immediately.
#
# The allowance above is first-come-first-served, so without this a handful of accounts
# drink all of it: at five each, four accounts empty a pool of twenty before anyone in
# another timezone is awake. Capping the individual spreads the same pool across more
# people and makes draining it cost more accounts.
# FIRST_DAY_EVAL_LIMIT="2"

# Max new accounts per client IP per UTC day. Defaults to 10 if unset; "0" closes
# registration. Raise it if your users share a NAT (a classroom, an office).
#
# The per-user evaluation limit assumes accounts are not free to mint. Without this they
# are: one script, unlimited accounts, and the whole day's ceiling gone before anyone
# else wakes up.
# REGISTRATIONS_PER_IP_LIMIT="10"
